WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.860
How do DAG shortest paths

2
00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.480
work? Process vertices in a

3
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.500
topological order and relax each

4
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.712
outgoing edge once. Unlike Dijkstra,

5
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.220
this schedule can handle negative

6
00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:12.860
edge weights because a DAG

7
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:14.160
has no directed cycles.

8
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.380
The shortest-path recording mentions this

9
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.360
linear-time DAG tutorial problem. We

10
00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:21.700
can derive the procedure by

11
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.920
combining the course’s topological ordering

12
00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.080
with its relaxation rule, rather

13
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.700
than inventing a different kind

14
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:28.180
of distance.

15
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.460
Use the topological order S,

16
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.640
A, B, T. Set distance

17
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.140
S to zero and the

18
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.200
rest to infinity; although A

19
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:37.460
to B has weight negative

20
00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.680
four, every arrow still points

21
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:40.920
forwards in the order.

22
00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.920
Process S first and relax

23
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.260
both outgoing edges. A receives

24
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:46.960
two and B receives five,

25
00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.960
with parent S; these are

26
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.240
the best routes seen so

27
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.100
far, not a finalisation by

28
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:52.940
smallest distance.

29
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.060
Next process A, even though

30
00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.360
a different graph might give

31
00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.160
another later vertex a smaller

32
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.320
label. A to B offers

33
00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.880
two minus four, improving B

34
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:05.800
to negative two, while A

35
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.320
to T offers nine.

36
00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.560
Process B next and relax

37
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:11.823
its edge to T. Negative

38
00:01:11.823 --> 00:01:13.700
two plus three equals one,

39
00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.740
improving T from nine to

40
00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.200
one and changing its parent

41
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:17.760
to B.

42
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.600
T has no outgoing edges,

43
00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.440
so the pass is complete.

44
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.740
The final route S, A,

45
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.980
B, T costs two minus

46
00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.820
four plus three, which is

47
00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:31.140
one; following the parents recovers

48
00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:31.360
it.

49
00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:33.780
Why can one pass suffice?

50
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.060
Every predecessor of a vertex

51
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.460
appears earlier in topological order,

52
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.040
so every possible final incoming

53
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.880
edge has already offered its

54
00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:44.840
candidate before that vertex is

55
00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:45.240
processed.

56
00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.220
Topological sorting takes order n

57
00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.020
plus m time with adjacency

58
00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.820
lists. Initialisation costs order n,

59
00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.440
and the subsequent pass scans

60
00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.060
every vertex and edge at

61
00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.040
most once, so total time

62
00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.680
remains order n plus m.

63
00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.580
Distances, parents, and the topological

64
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.780
order need order n auxiliary

65
00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:08.320
storage. A DFS-based topological sort

66
00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.580
may also use a linear

67
00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.900
stack; source removal can use

68
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.995
an indegree array and worklist

69
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:14.180
instead.

70
00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:16.360
Things to take note of.

71
00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:19.520
Verify acyclicity before relying on

72
00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.260
this schedule, skip arithmetic from

73
00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.860
unreachable infinity labels, and process

74
00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.600
all vertices in a valid

75
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.720
topological order. Sorting vertices by

76
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.220
tentative distance is a different

77
00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:31.760
algorithm.

78
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.120
Negative edges are allowed because

79
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.220
there is no cycle to

80
00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.400
revisit, not because negative weights

81
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.600
are harmless everywhere. A singleton

82
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.500
source stays at zero, and

83
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.600
unreachable vertices stay infinite. Use

84
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.680
the graph’s dependency order, and

85
00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.300
repeated passes disappear.
